{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a159bcaa513a365e","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9016\n3\tepic_count: 4\n4\tcritical_path_length: 3\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\trevised: false\n7\ttriage: null\n8\ttriage_rationale: null\n9\t---\n10\t# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-9016: Idle resource reporting in the web console\n11\t\n12\tTriage (Step 0): neither check fired. The strategy is not below threshold —\n13\tit spans three components (web console, notebook controller, Kubeflow\n14\tPipelines) owned by three distinct teams, and the scope contains multiple\n15\tarchitecturally distinct work streams (console UI reporting over two\n16\tdisparate data sources, plus substantive documentation). It is not\n17\tdocumentation-only — code changes are required in the console. Full\n18\tdecomposition (Steps 1-8) was executed.\n19\t\n20\t## Step 1 Parse Notes\n21\t\n22\tThe strategy is compact: HLR table, affected-component list, out-of-scope\n23\tlist, and an explicit \"Open Questions: None\". Health warnings for missing\n24\tsections are recorded below (Health Warnings).\n25\t\n26\t## Epic List\n27\t\n28\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n29\t|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n30\t| RHAISTRAT-9016-E001 | Notebook activity data source for idle-workbench reporting | Implementation | Workbenches | P1 |\n31\t| RHAISTRAT-9016-E002 | Per-project idle-workbench report in the web console | Implementation | RHOAI Dashboard | P1 |\n32\t| RHAISTRAT-9016-E003 | Stale pipeline-run report in the web console | Implementation | RHOAI Dashboard | P1 |\n33\t| RHAISTRAT-9016-E004 | Documentation for the idle and stale resource reports | Implementation (docs-authoring) | RHOAI Dashboard | P2 |\n34\t\n35\t## Dependency DAG\n36\t\n37\t```mermaid\n38\tgraph TD\n39\t    E001 --> E002\n40\t    E001 --> E004\n41\t    E002 --> E004\n42\t    E003 --> E004\n43\t```\n44\t\n45\tCritical path: E001 → E002 → E004 (length 3).\n46\t\n47\t## DAG Justification\n48\t\n49\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n50\t|------|------|-----------|\n51\t| E001 → E002 | Rule 13 (artifact consumer) | E001 produces the consumable last-activity data source (a surface on the Notebook CR) that the idle-workbench report's code reads; the console report builds against it, so the epics are serial. |\n52\t| E001 → E004 | Rule 11 (docs last) | Documentation must describe what was built; docs-authoring is blocked by every Implementation epic. |\n53\t| E002 → E004 | Rule 11 (docs last) | Same — documentation of the idle-workbench report is blocked by the report's implementation. |\n54\t| E003 → E004 | Rule 11 (docs last) | Same — documentation of the stale pipeline-run report is blocked by the report's implementation. |\n55\t| (no edge) E001 ∥ E003 | Rule 14 | Different repos and components with no shared artifacts — the stale-run report reads KFP run metadata, not notebook activity data. Parallel. |\n56\t| (no edge) E002 ∥ E003 | Rule 15 | Same repo (console), different areas (workbench report vs. pipeline-run report), no shared data structures or API endpoints. Parallel; merge coordination is a review-time concern, not a dependency. |\n57\t\n58\t## Component Mapping and Passive-Component Handling\n59\t\n60\tCanonical component names (from `.context/rhai-components.txt`) mapped from\n61\tthe strategy's Affected Components list:\n62\t\n63\t| Strategy component | Canonical component | Owning team (from architecture context) |\n64\t|--------------------|----------------------|------------------------------------------|\n65\t| RHOAI web console | Dashboard | RHOAI Dashboard |\n66\t| Notebook controller | Notebooks | Workbenches |\n67\t| Kubeflow Pipelines | Data Science Pipelines | RHOAI Pipelines |\n68\t\n69\tActive/passive classification:\n70\t\n71\t- **Dashboard — active.** All new report UI and CSV export code lives here;\n72\t  generates E002 and E003.\n73\t- **Notebooks (notebook controller) — passive, validated by a different\n74\t  team.** The controller already tracks last activity (architecture context:\n75\t  `notebooks.md` — \"last activity tracked per kernel through Jupyter server\n76\t  API probes\", consumed internally by the idle culler). No culling-behavior\n77\t  change is requested. Per the Step 2 exception, because the consuming epic\n78\t  is owned by the RHOAI Dashboard team while the data source is owned by the\n79\t  Workbenches team, a separate validation/exposure Implementation epic (E001)\n80\t  is generated and owned by Workbenches. Its deliverable is a supported,\n81\t  consumable interface to the activity data (for example surfaced on the\n82\t  Notebook CR); the concrete exposure mechanism is an implementation decision\n83\t  for that team.\n84\t- **Data Science Pipelines — passive, validated as an acceptance criterion.**\n85\t  Run completion metadata is reachable through the documented KFP v2 REST\n86\t  API, an interface the console already consumes to surface pipeline runs\n87\t  (`data-science-pipelines.md`). No separate cross-team validation epic is\n88\t  needed: validation that the report reconciles with KFP run metadata is an\n89\t  acceptance criterion on E003.\n90\t\n91\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n92\t\n93\t| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) | Notes |\n94\t|-----|----------|---------|-------|\n95\t| HLR-1 | P1 | E002 | Per-project idle-workbench report (last activity, owner, reserved resources) sourced from notebook-controller activity tracking; enabled by the E001 data source. |\n96\t| HLR-2 | P1 | E003 | Stale pipeline-run report (completed runs older than a configurable age with retained artifacts) sourced from KFP run metadata. |\n97\t| HLR-3 | P2 | E002, E003 | **bundled** — CSV export renders the same report data on the same report views that the P1 work builds; each export shares the data structure and UI surface of its report, so splitting it out would double-touch the same surfaces and create an artificial serialization edge. Export ships as scope of each report epic. |\n98\t| HLR-4 | P2 | E004 | Substantive documentation authoring (new doc topics on the reports and safe reclamation steps) — separate `docs-authoring` epic per the work-product-type boundary. |\n99\t\n100\tAll four HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements.\n101\t\n102\t## Priority Inheritance\n103\t\n104\t- E001 enables HLR-1 (P1) work and inherits P1.\n105\t- E002 and E003 directly implement P1 HLRs.\n106\t- E004 is `docs-authoring`: exempt from priority inheritance; the strategy's\n107\t  frontmatter `priority: Normal` maps to P2.\n108\t\n109\t## Health Warnings\n110\t\n111\t- **Missing strategy sections**: the strategy has no Impacted Teams table, no\n112\t  Dependencies table, no Given/When/Then acceptance criteria, and no\n113\t  non-functional requirements section. Teams were inferred from the\n114\t  architecture-context owning-team fields, and epic acceptance criteria were\n115\t  derived from the HLR wording. Non-blocking; the decomposition proceeded\n116\t  with available information.\n117\t- **Docs epic team assumption**: the Documentation component has no\n118\t  architecture-context file, so no owning team is on record. The docs epic is\n119\t  assigned to the RHOAI Dashboard team (owner of the reported-on feature);\n120\t  reassign if a dedicated documentation team owns this content. Non-blocking.\n121\t- **Configurability scope**: HLR-2's \"configurable age\" — where the staleness\n122\t  threshold is configured (per-project admin setting vs. deployment-level\n123\t  default) is an implementation decision for the Dashboard team, captured as\n124\t  an acceptance criterion on E003, not a DAG-gating unknown.\n125\t- **Epic size**: no epic exceeds the ~2-week epic guideline; each epic is a\n126\t  single logical change.\n127\t\n128\t## Tiered Delivery\n129\t\n130\tNot applicable. There are no external dependencies with uncertain timing —\n131\tall components are in the platform (architecture context files exist for\n132\tDashboard, Notebooks, and Data Science Pipelines, all GA, all Konflux\n133\tonboarded), so no onboarding chain and no tiered split is required.\n134\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b96e559e4074efe0","agent_id":"abc16d25dd159bf8f","session_id":"accbcd91-65ae-42f1-87a7-23c32a932432","uuid":"9647ad09-9348-4ac8-a3c5-80d5681081bb","timestamp":"2026-10-25T18:54:09.325Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-a159bcaa513a365e","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-9016 decomposition"}
